From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 13:10:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 25/79] fix(refinery): make the duplicate check use a verb that
 exists and fail closed (gcp-ex1s)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mol-refinery-patrol's handle-failures step prescribed this before filing a
pre-existing-failure bead:

    gc bd list --type=bug --status=open --search "<failure summary>"

`bd list` has no `--search` flag. The command exits non-zero with
`unknown flag: --search`, and piped to jq or head that yields empty output —
which an agent correctly reads as "no duplicate exists", whereupon the step's
next instruction is "If no duplicate: file a new bead". The gate has never
searched anything, and it failed open every single time. That is what produced
six open beads for the same seven go1.26.5 govulncheck stdlib vulns in winnow,
and ~10 open beads for the same test_no_bare_bd_commands failure in this rig.

Three things were wrong, and fixing only the flag would have left the gate
inert:

- The verb. `gc bd search "<keyword>"` is what actually queries titles.
- The query shape. `gc bd search` matches titles, so pasting a whole failure
  sentence returns [] — exit 0, empty, indistinguishable from a real miss.
  Verified: the sentence form returns [] while the keyword `govulncheck` or
  `test_no_bare_bd_commands` returns every duplicate. The step now demands a
  distinctive keyword (failing test name, tool name, CVE id), and requires that
  keyword in the title of any bead it files, so the next patrol can find it.
- The failure direction. The check now captures the exit code and jq-parses the
  result; a non-zero exit or unparseable output halts and escalates to
  mayor + witness instead of falling through to `gc bd create`. An unverified
  duplicate check is not evidence of no duplicate.

Also adds a regression guard to the formula/prompt drift suite, which already
exists to stop exactly this class of lossy agent-facing command from
reappearing. The detector requires the `--search` to sit in the argument run of
a `bd list` invocation with no backtick between them, which catches both a
fenced command and a quick-reference table cell quoting one whole, while
leaving prose that names the broken flag in separate inline-code spans
writable — otherwise this commit message's own explanation could not be
committed to a scanned asset.

Validation: guard cycled red (pinpoints mol-refinery-patrol.toml:426 on the
pre-fix tree) then green. The corrected block was extracted from the committed
TOML and executed against the live rig with every mutating verb stubbed: a real
keyword returned all 11 open duplicates and filed nothing; a genuine miss
returned 0; a forced `unknown flag: --search` error halted with exit 1, mailed
mayor, nudged witness, drain-acked, and created no bead. Full CI pytest set is
8 failed / 779 passed against a byte-identical 8 failed / 778 passed baseline —
no new failures; the 8 (test_no_bare_bd_commands, gascity formula assets,
discord/github intake) are pre-existing on integration and already tracked.
gastown shell tests 9/9, go vet/build/test -race green, registry.toml ok.
---
 gastown/formulas/mol-refinery-patrol.toml  |  44 ++++++++-
 tests/test_prompt_formula_command_drift.py | 106 +++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 145 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gastown/formulas/mol-refinery-patrol.toml b/gastown/formulas/mol-refinery-patrol.toml
index 6dee9b0..0056d20 100644
--- a/gastown/formulas/mol-refinery-patrol.toml
+++ b/gastown/formulas/mol-refinery-patrol.toml
@@ -421,14 +421,48 @@ TARGET=$(gc bd show $WORK --json | jq -r '.[0].metadata.target // "{{target_bran
    - Close this step after the current wisp is burned.
 
 3. If pre-existing on target:
-   - Check for duplicates first:
+   - Check for duplicates FIRST, and run the whole block — the check has to
+     fail closed. `gc bd list` has no `--search` flag; it exits non-zero with
+     `unknown flag: --search`, which piped to jq yields empty output that reads
+     exactly like "no duplicate exists". That is how one pre-existing failure
+     becomes ten identical open bug beads. `gc bd search` is the verb that
+     actually queries titles.
      ```bash
-     gc bd list --type=bug --status=open --search "<failure summary>"
+     # Search a DISTINCTIVE KEYWORD, not the failure sentence: `gc bd search`
+     # matches against titles, so a whole summary matches nothing and returns
+     # [] — indistinguishable from a genuine miss. Use the failing test name,
+     # the tool name, the CVE id (e.g. test_no_bare_bd_commands, govulncheck).
+     DUP_KEYWORD="<distinctive keyword from the failure>"
+     DUP_ERR=$(mktemp)
+     DUP_JSON=$(gc bd search "$DUP_KEYWORD" --type=bug --status=open --json 2>"$DUP_ERR")
+     DUP_CODE=$?
+     DUP_MSG=$(cat "$DUP_ERR"); rm -f "$DUP_ERR"
+     DUP_COUNT=$(printf '%s' "$DUP_JSON" | jq 'length' 2>/dev/null)
+     if [ "$DUP_CODE" -ne 0 ] || [ -z "$DUP_COUNT" ]; then
+       # FAIL CLOSED. An errored or unparseable duplicate check is NOT
+       # "no duplicate found" — filing here is the exact shape that produced
+       # the duplicate pile-ups. Halt and escalate; do NOT create a bead.
+       gc mail send mayor/ -s "ESCALATION: refinery duplicate check failed, not filing [HIGH]" -m "Work bead: $WORK
+     Keyword: $DUP_KEYWORD
+     Exit code: $DUP_CODE
+     Stderr: $DUP_MSG
+     Refused to file a pre-existing-failure bead on an unverified duplicate check."
+       gc session nudge "${GC_RIG:+$GC_RIG/}{{binding_prefix}}witness" "DUP-CHECK HALT: $WORK — gc bd search failed (code $DUP_CODE); refused to file. Merge not attempted."
+       echo "STOP. Duplicate check did not run. NEVER fall through to filing."
+       gc runtime drain-ack
+       exit 1
+     fi
+     if [ "$DUP_COUNT" -gt 0 ]; then
+       printf '%s' "$DUP_JSON" | jq -r '.[] | "DUPLICATE \\(.id)  \\(.title)"'
+     fi
      ```
-   - If a matching bug already exists: note its ID and proceed with merge.
-   - If no duplicate: file a new bead:
+   - If `DUP_COUNT` > 0: note the matching bead ID and proceed with merge. Do
+     not file.
+   - If `DUP_COUNT` is 0 — a check that ran and genuinely found nothing — file
+     a new bead. Put `$DUP_KEYWORD` in the title verbatim, or the next patrol's
+     search cannot find what you just filed:
      ```bash
-     gc bd create --type=bug --priority=1 --title="Pre-existing failure: <summary>"
+     gc bd create --type=bug --priority=1 --title="Pre-existing failure: $DUP_KEYWORD — <summary>"
      ```
    - Proceed with merge (failure not caused by this branch)
 
diff --git a/tests/test_prompt_formula_command_drift.py b/tests/test_prompt_formula_command_drift.py
index 133fbb3..573d341 100644
--- a/tests/test_prompt_formula_command_drift.py
+++ b/tests/test_prompt_formula_command_drift.py
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@ import subprocess
 
 
 REPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
+THIS_FILE = Path(__file__).resolve()
 AGENT_PROMPT_GLOB = "gastown/agents/*/prompt.template.md"
 
 FENCE_OPEN = re.compile(r"^\s*```(\w*)\s*$")
@@ -33,6 +34,27 @@ FENCE_CLOSE = re.compile(r"^\s*```\s*$")
 
 POOL_RETURN_ASSIGNEE = re.compile(r"""--assignee=(?:""|'')""")
 
+# `bd list ... --search`, with no backtick in between. The backtick clause is
+# what separates an invocation from the prose warning against one: a sentence
+# naming the broken flag puts `gc bd list` and `--search` in two different
+# inline-code spans, while a real command — fenced, or quoted whole in a
+# quick-reference table cell — has nothing but arguments between them.
+BD_LIST_SEARCH = re.compile(r"\bbd\s+list\b[^`\n]*--search")
+
+SCANNED_SUFFIXES = frozenset({".md", ".toml", ".sh"})
+
+
+def tracked_command_assets() -> list[Path]:
+    """Every tracked file in the repo that can carry an agent-facing command."""
+    result = subprocess.run(
+        ["git", "ls-files", "-z"],
+        cwd=REPO_ROOT,
+        check=True,
+        capture_output=True,
+    )
+    paths = [REPO_ROOT / part for part in result.stdout.decode().split("\0") if part]
+    return [path for path in paths if path.suffix in SCANNED_SUFFIXES]
+
 
 def gastown_assets() -> list[Path]:
     """Every tracked pack asset that can carry an agent-facing command."""
@@ -164,6 +186,34 @@ def bail_without_drain_violations(path: Path, text: str) -> list[str]:
     return violations
 
 
+def bd_list_search_violations(path: Path, text: str) -> list[str]:
+    """`bd list` has no `--search` flag, and the failure mode is invisible.
+
+    `gc bd list --search "<query>"` does not search weakly — it never runs.
+    It exits non-zero with ``unknown flag: --search``, and piped to jq or head
+    that yields empty output, which the caller correctly reads as "nothing
+    matched". Every duplicate check written this way reports a clean miss.
+
+    That is not hypothetical either: mol-refinery-patrol's handle-failures step
+    prescribed exactly this before filing a pre-existing-failure bead, so the
+    dedup gate had never once searched anything. It produced six open beads for
+    the same seven govulncheck stdlib vulns in the winnow rig, and ~10 open
+    beads for the same test_no_bare_bd_commands failure in this one.
+
+    The verbs that actually query titles are ``gc bd search "<keyword>"`` and
+    ``gc bd list --title-contains "<keyword>"``.
+    """
+    violations = []
+    for number, command in logical_commands(text):
+        if not BD_LIST_SEARCH.search(command):
+            continue
+        violations.append(
+            f"{path.relative_to(REPO_ROOT)}:{number}: bd list has no --search flag "
+            "(use `gc bd search` or `gc bd list --title-contains`)"
+        )
+    return violations
+
+
 def test_pool_returning_updates_declare_routing() -> None:
     violations = []
     for path in gastown_assets():
@@ -202,6 +252,24 @@ def test_prompt_template_bail_paths_drain_ack() -> None:
     )
 
 
+def test_no_asset_queries_beads_with_bd_list_search() -> None:
+    violations = []
+    for path in tracked_command_assets():
+        if path.resolve() == THIS_FILE:
+            continue
+        try:
+            text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
+        except UnicodeDecodeError:
+            continue
+        violations.extend(bd_list_search_violations(path, text))
+
+    assert not violations, (
+        "a duplicate check written with --search never runs: it errors, yields "
+        "empty output, and the caller files a duplicate bead believing it "
+        "checked:\n" + "\n".join(violations)
+    )
+
+
 def test_detectors_catch_the_drift_they_are_named_for() -> None:
     fixture = REPO_ROOT / "fixture.md"
 
@@ -256,3 +324,41 @@ def test_detectors_catch_the_drift_they_are_named_for() -> None:
     assert not bail_without_drain_violations(
         fixture, "```bash\necho no\ngc runtime drain-ack\nexit 1\n```"
     )
+
+    # Built line-by-line for the same reason as the warrant fixtures above: a
+    # literal backslash-n immediately before `gc` reads as a bare `bd`
+    # invocation to tests/test_no_bare_bd_commands.py's line-based scanner.
+    assert bd_list_search_violations(
+        fixture, 'gc bd list --type=bug --status=open --search "<failure summary>"'
+    )
+    # Continuation-joined, the form a formula is most likely to grow into.
+    assert bd_list_search_violations(
+        fixture,
+        "\n".join(
+            [
+                "gc bd list \\",
+                "  --type=bug \\",
+                '  --search "govulncheck"',
+            ]
+        ),
+    )
+    # The two verbs that actually query titles.
+    assert not bd_list_search_violations(
+        fixture, 'gc bd search "govulncheck" --type=bug --status=open --json'
+    )
+    assert not bd_list_search_violations(
+        fixture, 'gc bd list --status=open --title-contains "govulncheck"'
+    )
+    # `gh` genuinely has --search; only the beads CLI is being constrained.
+    assert not bd_list_search_violations(
+        fixture, 'gh issue list --repo gastownhall/gascity --search "<keywords>"'
+    )
+    # A quick-reference table cell quotes the whole command, so it is caught...
+    assert bd_list_search_violations(
+        fixture, '| Dedup check | `gc bd list --type=bug --search "<summary>"` |'
+    )
+    # ...but prose warning against the flag names the two halves in separate
+    # inline-code spans, and must stay writable.
+    assert not bd_list_search_violations(
+        fixture, "`gc bd list` has no `--search` flag; use `gc bd search` instead."
+    )
